lib/File/SOPS.pm POD (the "Value types" and "Saying what a value is" sections) shows

    File::SOPS->encrypt(data => { v => JSON->true })   # type:bool
    $data->{flag}  = JSON->true;                       # type:bool

as the only documented way to produce type:bool, and never says the caller has to `use JSON::MaybeXS;` for it.

File::SOPS re-exports nothing -- `use namespace::clean` strips encode_json/decode_json/JSON from the package, verified:

    perl -Ilib -MFile::SOPS -e "print defined &File::SOPS::JSON ? 1 : 0"   # 0

but the example nevertheless works after a bare `use File::SOPS;`:

    perl -Ilib -MFile::SOPS -e "print ref(JSON->true)"   # JSON::PP::Boolean

by accident. `JSON->true` there is not the JSON::MaybeXS-exported JSON() sub, it is a class-method call on package JSON, which is in %INC only because CryptX loads JSON.pm (CryptX.pm line 14) on the way to Crypt::AuthEnc::GCM. Nothing in this distribution asks for JSON.pm, and nothing keeps CryptX doing it.

Fix: say in the POD that the boolean examples need `use JSON::MaybeXS;` in the CALLER (JSON::MaybeXS is already a prereq via the format handlers), or show a form that does not rely on an imported bareword. Either way the example should stop being true only by luck.

